seg-36bad4b7b4ba = the Newtonian one-way light time, solved by the radiometric fixed-point light-time solver with the transmitter at its retarded position, and the corresponding one-way range;

# text
seg-c5602eb76a05 = the two-way light time and range;

# text
seg-dab8e2db61d3 = the Sun's Shapiro delay, reported separately and not added;

# text
seg-90d00587a59f = the Sun separation angle.

# text
seg-4c172f9f7cf6 = The light time is VALIDATED against the one-way light time Horizons reports: Mars and Jupiter to the Earth's centre at four epochs from { "2000" } to { "2040" }, worst error { "0.070" } s for Mars against a { "0.207" } s bar and { "1.31" } s for Jupiter against { "4.04" } s. The bar is twice the sum of the Standish distance errors of the target and the Earth-Moon barycentre, divided by the speed of light: the light time can be no better than the positions. It is the Newtonian geometric light time, not a relativistic or plasma-corrected observable.

